Every quarter, another B2B team rolls out a new scoring model in HubSpot. Properties get added. Automations get wired up. A slide deck goes out. Reps nod.

By month two, nobody's using it.

The failure isn't the model. It's the rollout. Three patterns, in order of how often we see them.

**The score nobody sees.**
The scoring model lives in a custom property on the contact or company record. Nobody's default view shows it. Reps never open it, so they never factor it in. The score exists in the database and nowhere else.

**The score that contradicts the rep's gut.**
A rep knows their territory. If the scoring model says "work this account" and the rep has a reason to believe it's a dead end, they'll trust themselves. If the model contradicts them too often without explanation, they stop checking it.

**The score that changes too much.**
If an account's score jumps by 40 points overnight because a single property changed, reps learn to ignore movement. Scoring is only useful if it's stable enough to plan a week around.

The fix for all three is the same: make the score **load-bearing, explainable, and stable**.

Load-bearing means it shows up where the rep already works — on the account record, in the weekly prioritized list, in the dashboard they already open. If a rep has to click into a different view to see the score, the score is optional. Optional signals get ignored.

Explainable means a score shouldn't just be a number. It should say *why*. "Score 87: Fit 30, Intent 40 (pricing page visit 3d ago), Timing 17 (contract renewal in 45 days)." A rep who sees the reason can decide whether it matches what they already know. If it does, trust builds. If it doesn't, at least they know what the model thought.

Stable means day-to-day changes are small. Big changes should be tied to events you can name: a new intent signal, a funding event, a renewal window opening. Not to a mass recalculation triggered by a field update on ten thousand accounts.

Scoring that's load-bearing, explainable, and stable gets used. Scoring that isn't, dies in month two. Every time.
